# Can't Access Global Variable Inside Function

Attempting to modify a variable defined in the global scope from within a function using that same name is a common point of confusion for developers, particularly when dealing with recursive logic or complex object graphs. The snippet provided in the source material illustrates this perfectly: defining `$sxml` at the script level and trying to use it inside `foo()`.

In PHP, variables defined outside of functions exist in the global scope. However, a function creates its own local scope. When you declare a variable with the same name inside a function, that variable shadows the global one, or if you don't declare it at all, the function expects it to be passed as an argument or rely on `global` keywords.

The core issue in the first example is simply that `$sxml` is not visible inside `foo()` by default. PHP does not automatically promote global variables into local scopes to prevent accidental modification of state. To access `$sxml` without passing it explicitly, you must declare it as a global variable inside the function using the `global` keyword:

```php
<?php
$sxml = new SimpleXMLElement('<somexml/>');

function foo() {
    global $sxml; // Promotes the global $sxml into local scope

    if ($sxml->addChild('child')) {
        echo "Success";
    } else {
        // Log errors on $sxml here if foo fails
    }
}

foo();
?>
```

While using `global` works, it is often considered a code smell in larger applications because it tightly couples the function to the global state. This makes testing difficult and can lead to bugs where one part of the application overwrites a variable expected by another. However, for simple scripts or utility functions where you strictly need to avoid argument passing overhead, this is a valid approach.

The second scenario in your source material introduces a different problem entirely: variable scoping within nested function definitions.

```php
<?php
function bar() {
    $sxml = new SimpleXMLElement('<somexml/>');

    function foo() {
        // $sxml is NOT accessible here!
        $child = $sxml->addChild('child'); 
    }

    foo();
}
bar();
?>
```

In this case, even if you add `global $sxml` inside `foo()`, you will likely get a "Cannot access local variable in global scope" error. This happens because `$sxml` is not a true global variable; it is a local variable of the `bar()` function. When PHP parses `foo()`, it looks for globals, finds none (since `$sxml` belongs to `bar`), and throws an error upon trying to access it via the `global` keyword.

To make this work, you must pass the context explicitly or use closures that capture variables from their parent scope. Since PHP 5.3, closures can use the `use` operator to grab variables from the surrounding scope of `bar()`. This is generally the preferred modern approach because it keeps data encapsulated within the function hierarchy rather than polluting the global namespace.

```php
function bar() {
    $sxml = new SimpleXMLElement('<somexml/>');

    $foo = function() use (&$sxml) {
        // & is needed for reference if we modify $sxml later, 
        // though addChild returns a new node usually.
        $child = $sxml->addChild('child');

        // Now if foo fails, we can still access $sxml via closure logic
    };

    $foo();
}
```

Regarding your concern about performance: passing `$sxml` as an argument (`foo($sxml)`) or using a closure with `use` is actually more efficient than using the `global` keyword. The `global` statement forces PHP to perform a lookup in the global symbol table every time the function is entered, which adds a slight overhead compared to accessing a local variable passed as an argument. In high-frequency recursive calls, like a directory listing algorithm, minimizing global lookups can be beneficial.

If you are working within a Laravel application, dependency injection via Service Providers or Constructor Injection is the standard pattern for handling objects like `SimpleXMLElement` (or its modern replacements). Passing the root element into your recursive helper methods ensures that your logic remains testable and isolated from the framework's global state management. For more complex XML manipulation needs in PHP, consider looking into dedicated libraries available at [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) which might offer higher-level abstractions to avoid manual DOM node management entirely.
